rent from being able to reach it.</p><p>=E2=80=8D</p><p>The rules were conf=
using. The forms were difficult. The information was not accessible. Each t=
ime he tried to understand what he was entitled to, he met another closed d=
oor. What should have been a public service felt like a maze.</p><p>=E2=80=
=8D</p><p>An OPD coalition in Cambodia began producing public information i=
n formats people could actually use: easy-read, audio, Khmer Sign Language =
video, and plain language. The materials explained disability benefits, sch=
ool enrolment, health rights and how to report violence.</p><p>=E2=80=8D</p=
><p>For Dara, accessible information changed everything. He understood the =
steps. He knew what documents he needed. He contacted the OPD for follow-up=
 support and was able to apply for services that had previously felt out of=
 reach.</p><p>=E2=80=8D</p><p>Accessible information is often treated as an=
 add-on. For Dara, it was the bridge between having rights on paper and bei=
ng able to use them in real life.</p></div></div></div></div></article></di=
